<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="65203" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/ANTH/499/65203.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="ANTH" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/ANTH.xml">Anthropology</subject><course id="499" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/ANTH/499.xml">Topics in Anthropology</course></parents><sectionNumber>LD1</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Computational Gene Genealogy</sectionTitle><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This course equips students with computational tools to analyze familial lineages. Using Python, Java, and Bash-based genetic analyses, students will explore family structures and genetic genealogy inferences spanning the last seven generations. The course emphasizes practical applications in anthropology, enabling students to assist in the analysis and interpretation of complex genetic genealogy studies. Students will learn to apply these skills in the real-world historical context of mass transatlantic human trafficking from Africa.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>338</roomNumber><buildingName>Davenport Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="David" firstName="L">David, L</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>